    {
      "slug": "reddit-email-everything",
      "script": "Email everything? Fine. My new supervisor hated 'wasting time' on hallway questions, so he posted a memo: every request goes through email, no exceptions, effective immediately. I stopped answering anyone who just walked up to my desk. Day one, seven emails piled into his inbox before lunch—each a two-line question he once waved off in ten seconds. By day four, ninety-one unread messages waited, plus a folder titled 'URGENT' with fourteen items, half from himself asking where things were. Very efficient. Day five brought an all-staff meeting: verbal requests were, actually, fine again, especially small ones, and please stop CC'ing him on literally everything. I printed his original memo, framed it, and hung it above my monitor. He passed it every single day and never once said a word. Who really won the email-everything memo?",
      "editor_note": "Punched up to a two-word question hook, front-loaded the email rule, added one dry efficiency beat, and landed on the memo while preserving every number, entity, shot phrase, and query."
    },
    {
      "slug": "reddit-dont-work-here",
      "script": "She thought I worked there? I was standing in the paint aisle for four minutes, in street clothes, holding my own basket, when a woman demanded to know why nobody had restocked the drop cloths. I told her, twice, I didn't work there. She said 'don't play dumb' and pointed at a shelf. So I became the single most confident employee that store never hired. I explained the drop cloths were discontinued; the good ones were two aisles over by the pet food, and the industrial size was 'in the back, by returns.' Extremely official. She thanked me and marched off toward dog treats. Ninety seconds later, an actual employee in a red vest passed. She demanded to know why his coworker misdirected her on purpose. He had no idea. I was already checking out. Who promoted the paint-aisle stranger?",
      "editor_note": "Punched up to a five-word question hook, tightened the fake-employee escalation, added one dry aside, and landed on the paint-aisle stranger while preserving every number, entity, shot phrase, and query."
    }
